  abstract={Early diagnosis of oral cancer is essential for improving survival rates and reducing complications. In this work, an automated image classification framework is developed using a deep learning approach to distinguish between malignant and non-malignant oral lesions. The system incorporates image preprocessing techniques including resizing to 224×224 pixels, normalization through pixel rescaling, and data augmentation such as rotation, zoom, brightness variation, and horizontal flipping to enhance model generalization and reduce overfitting. The model is trained using a two-stage process involving initial training with frozen base layers followed by fine-tuning to improve feature extraction. For prediction, a probability-based thresholding mechanism is applied, where outputs are categorized as malignant, benign, or uncertain, improving interpretability in borderline cases. In addition, Grad-CAM is utilized to visualize important regions in the input images, providing transparency and aiding clinical understanding of the model’s decisions. Experimental evaluation using validation accuracy, loss curves, confusion matrix, and classification report demonstrates stable convergence and reliable performance of the trained model.The proposed system provides an efficient and interpretable solution for computer-aided oral cancer screening and has the potential to be deployed in real-time and resource-constrained healthcare environments.},
