  number={},
  pages={1-7},
  abstract={Accurate, early diagnosis of dental diseases such as caries, gingivitis, and tooth discoloration is critical for effective treatment and the prevention of long-term complications. Despite the convolutional neural networks’ (CNNs) potential in medical image analysis, single-model methodologies frequently exhibit insufficient resilience and generalizability. Adopting a late-fusion strategy, this study integrates DenseNet121 and InceptionV3 into an ensemble deep learning framework for multi-class dental disease classification. The convolutional bases of both models are partially frozen to maintain most of the layers as nontrainable and thus preserve the low- and mid-level feature representations. The last 50 layers are unfrozen and fine-tuned to adapt to task-specific dental features. Custom classification heads comprising of global average pooling, dense layers with corrected linear unit activation, batch normalization, dropout regularization, and a SoftMax output were optimized for the target task. To enhance its robustness, the framework incorporates extensive data augmentation, class balancing with weights, and focal loss as an optional optimization strategy. The impact of architectural complexity on model performance was systematically evaluated across classification heads with varying depths (1–4 dense layers). On both validation and test datasets the proposed ensemble consistently outperformed the individual models in terms of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score, achieving an impressive accuracy of 88.24%, a recall of 88.24%, a precision of 88.67%, and an F1-score of 88.32% on the test set. Furthermore, explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) was incorporated using Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping, providing interpretable visual explanations of the discriminative regions contributing to the predictions. Besides enhancing clinical trust in the framework, these visual insights validate the ensemble’s focus on pathology-relevant areas within dental images. Combining the complementary strengths of DenseNet121 and InceptionV3, systematic architectural exploration, and XAI, the proposed framework is a potentially reliable and interpretable tool for computer-assisted dental diagnostics and advanced dental-image classification.},
