  booktitle={2025 3rd International Conference on Cyber Resilience (ICCR)}, 
  title={Prostate Cancer Volume Segmentation in Multiparametric MRI Scans Using Optimized 3D Dense CNN Architectures}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-8},
  abstract={This research proposes an optimized 3D Dense Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architecture for high-precision prostate cancer volume segmentation in multi-parametric MRI (mpMRI), incorporating modality-aware feature fusion, deep supervision, and hybrid loss optimization. Using a densely connected encoder-decoder backbone, enhanced with skip attention and multi-scale context encoding, the framework was evaluated across clinical-grade datasets including PROMISE12, PROSTATEx, and private institutional cohorts. The model consistently delivered segmentation accuracies above 88.9% Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC), with HD95 distances reduced to as low as 5.8 mm, validating its anatomical precision across whole gland, peripheral, and transition zones. Compared to conventional 2D/3D CNN baselines, the proposed system achieved a 4.5–6.5% improvement in accuracy, particularly in heterogeneous and scanner-variant settings. The inclusion of Dice-Focal hybrid loss and dense feature propagation enabled robust boundary preservation even in anatomically ambiguous regions. With an average inference latency under 500ms per patient volume, the model supports real-time deployment in clinical workflows. Its architecture-agnostic design ensures compatibility with future imaging modalities, scanner types, and automated radiology pipelines without reliance on handcrafted features or manual zone delineation.},
  keywords={Image segmentation;Solid modeling;Three-dimensional displays;Accuracy;Sensitivity;Magnetic resonance imaging;Robustness;Convolutional neural networks;Prostate cancer;Standards;Prostate Cancer Segmentation;Multiparametric MRI;3D Dense CNN;Deep Supervision;Modality Fusion;Medical Image Analysis;Hybrid Loss;Clinical AI},
